  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where:\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨\\frac{3-8s}{10},\\,\\frac{6s+4}{10}⟩,\\,s\\ge 0\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3-8s}{10}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{6s+4}{10})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{8}{10},\\,\\frac{6}{10}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\frac{3}{5}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(0)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
